st import the module and call the function \u2014 yes.\n\nLet me now also double-check the `WHITESPACE.match(s, end)` behavior when end > len(s): `re.Pattern.match(string, pos)` \u2014 if pos > len(string), it matches empty string? Let me think: `re.compile(r' *').match(\"abc\", 5)` \u2014 pos beyond string length. Python's re allows pos up to len(string); if pos > len, I believe it returns None? Hmm. Actually, `pattern.match(string, pos)` with pos > len(string): The regex engine treats pos as the start; matching `[*]` (zero or more) at pos beyond end... Let me think. In CPython, `sre` checks `state.ptr` bounds. For `re.compile(r' *').match('abc', 5)`: I believe it returns a match (empty match) \u2014 actually no. Let me think carefully.\n\nTest: `re.compile(r'a*').match('aaa', 10)` \u2014 hmm. The `re` module: if pos > len(string), I think it returns None for patterns that can match empty? Actually no \u2014 I recall that `pattern.match(s, pos)` where pos > len(s) raises no error but returns None? Or returns an empty match?\n\nLet me reason from the sre engine: MATCH starts at state.ptr = pos. For a star of a char class, it tries to match zero occurrences first \u2014 success without touching ptr. Then the match ends. Does the engine validate pos <= end at start? I don't think there's such a check; the empty match at pos > end would succeed. Hmm, but actually there IS a check: in `SRE_MATCH`, if `state->ptr > state->end`? Not sure.\n\nLet me test mentally with known behavior: `re.compile(r'').match('abc', 5)` \u2014 I'm fairly sure this returns a match object (empty match at pos 5)? Hmm, actually I recall `re.compile(r'').match('abc', 10)` returns `<re.Match object; span=(10, 10), match=''>`. Hmm wait, can span start be beyond the string? Let me think about `re.compile(r'x?').match('abc', 5)`.\n\nActually, I remember that in Python, `pattern.match(string, pos)` with pos beyond the string length returns None in some cases... Let me think about the actual C code. In `sre_lib.h`, `SRE_MATCH`:\n\n```c\nif (ctx->ptr > state->end) ... \n```\n\nHmm. Actually there's this in the dispatcher: `TRACE(...)` ... I recall that matching at pos > end: the general rule is that the subject is effectively truncated at state->end = len(string). If ptr starts beyond end... In `sre_match` setup: `state->ptr = state->start = string + pos;` and `state->end = string + len`. There's no explicit check that pos <= len. Then for LITERAL match, it checks `ptr < end` before reading \u2014 would fail. For empty match (star matching zero), success op just continues and ends with ptr = pos > end. The final `RETURN_SUCCESS` doesn't check ptr <= end I think. So it would return a match with span (pos, pos).\n\nHmm, but wait \u2014 in our code path, when does `_w(s, end)` get called with end > len(s)? Line 88: `end = _w(s, end).end()` \u2014 end here is after `end += 1` following nextchar=','. If the comma is the last char, end = len(s), and `_w(s, len(s))` \u2014 pos == len(s), which is fine (empty match at end). pos == len is definitely OK (`re.compile(r' *').match('abc', 3)` \u2192 match span (3,3)). pos > len only if... line 39: `end = _w(s, end).end()` \u2014 end \u2264 len(s) always there since nextchar = s[end:end+1] was non-empty? No wait \u2014 nextchar could be '' (empty slice) if end == len(s). Then `'' in _ws` \u2192 True (empty string is substring!). Then `_w(s, end)` with end == len(s) \u2192 fine. Then nextchar = s[end:end+1] = '' \u2192 not '}' and != '\"' \u2192 raise. OK so pos == len(s) max. Actually can end exceed len(s) at line 39? end comes from s_and_end; caller passes idx+1 where s[idx]=='{', so end \u2264 len(s). If end == len(s) (s == \"{\"), nextchar = '' \u2192 in _ws \u2192 _w(s, len) \u2192 empty match, end stays len. nextchar = '' \u2192 raise JSONDecodeError pos=len. Fine, no crash.\n\nLine 57: `end = _w(s, end).end()` \u2014 end \u2264 len(s) since s[end:end+1] was read. Fine.\n\nLine 66: `end = _w(s, end + 1).end()` \u2014 end+1 could exceed len(s)? Line 65: `if s[end] in _ws` \u2014 s[end] succeeded, so end < len(s), so end+1 \u2264 len(s). Fine.\n\nLine 78: `end = _w(s, end + 1).end()` \u2014 line 76 `nextchar = s[end]` succeeded \u2192 end < len(s) \u2192 end+1 \u2264 len(s). Fine.\n\nLine 88: end after `end += 1` at line 82. Before that, nextchar = s[end] or s[end:end+1]... At line 76, s[end] succeeded (or IndexError \u2192 nextchar=''). If s[end] succeeded, end \u2264 len-1, end+1 \u2264 len. If IndexError, nextchar='' \u2192 line 84/86 raise before line 88. So at line 88, end \u2264 len(s). `_w(s, end)` fine.\n\nOK so no re-with-pos-beyond-length issues in reachable paths. Good.\n\nNow, the fake scan_once for the truncated cases: at `{\"a\":`, after IndexError, scan_once(s, 5) with len(s)=5 \u2192 idx >= len \u2192 StopIteration(5). Good.\n\nNow let me write the final test file. Also consider adding a test that goes through the real module scanner for integration \u2014 let me first check get_info results.\n\nActually, let me also think about whether `ScalarToken` import in the test is needed \u2014 yes, for the fake scan_once and for position assertions.\n\nAlso: are Token instances hashable? `dict(pairs)` requires hashable keys. If Token defines `__eq__` without `__hash__`, it'd be unhashable and dict(pairs) would fail. But the real code works, so Token must be hashable (probably default identity hash, or custom). Let me check get_info for Token to see.\n\nLet me now make the tool calls.\n\nActually, one more design consideration: Should the tests call `_TokenizingJSONObject` with positional args matching the signature? Yes: `(s_and_end, strict, scan_once, memo, content)`. The `_w` and `_ws` defaults are used. Could also pass custom `_w`/`_ws` but defaults exercise the real WHITESPACE. Keep defaults.\n\nAlright, let me call get_info for: `_TokenizingJSONObject`, `ScalarToken`, `Token`, `_make_scanner`, `tokenize_json`. I'll batch them.\n\nThen finalize.\n\nLet me write out the expected test file now (to be adjusted after get_info):\n\n```python\nimport pytest\nfrom json.decoder import JSONDecodeError\n\nfrom typesystem.tokenize.tokenize_json import _TokenizingJSONObject\nfrom typesystem.tokenize.tokens import ScalarToken\n\n\ndef fake_scan_once(s, idx):\n    \"\"\"Minimal scanner stand-in: parses unsigned integer literals, nothing else.\"\"\"\n    if idx >= len(s) or not s[idx].isdigit():\n        raise StopIteration(idx)\n    end = idx\n    while end < len(s) and s[end].isdigit():\n        end += 1\n    return ScalarToken(int(s[idx:end]), idx, end - 1, s), end\n\n\ndef test_object_fast_path():\n    content = '{\"a\":1}'\n    result, end = _TokenizingJSONObject(\n        (content, 1), True, fake_scan_once, {}, content\n    )\n    assert end == len(content) == 7\n    assert list(result.keys())[0].value == \"a\"\n    key = list(result.keys())[0]\n    assert (key.start, key.end) == (1, 3)\n    value = list(result.values())[0]\n    assert value.value == 1\n    assert (value.start, value.end) == (5, 5)\n\n\ndef test_object_with_whitespace_around_keys_and_values():\n    content = '{ \"a\" : 1 , \"b\" : 2 }'\n    result, end = _TokenizingJSONObject(\n        (content, 1), True, fake_scan_once, {}, content\n    )\n    assert end == len(content) == 21\n    keys = list(result.keys())\n    assert [k.value for k in keys] == [\"a\", \"b\"]\n    assert [(k.start, k.end) for k in keys] == [(2, 4), (12, 14)]\n    assert [result[k].value for k in keys] == [1, 2]\n\n\ndef test_empty_object():\n    content = \"{}\"\n    result, end = _TokenizingJSONObject((content, 1), True, fake_scan_once, {}, content)\n    assert result == {}\n    assert end == 2\n\n\ndef test_empty_object_with_whitespace():\n    content = \"{ }\"\n    result, end = _TokenizingJSONObject((content, 1), True, fake_scan_once, {}, content)\n    assert result == {}\n    assert end == 3\n\n\ndef test_invalid_first_character():\n    content = \"{,}\"\n    with pytest.raises(JSONDecodeError) as excinfo:\n        _TokenizingJSONObject((content, 1), True, fake_scan_once, {}, content)\n    assert excinfo.value.msg == \"Expecting property name enclosed in double quotes\"\n    assert excinfo.value.pos == 1\n\n\ndef test_missing_colon_delimiter():\n    content = '{\"a\" 1}'\n    with pytest.raises(JSONDecodeError) as excinfo:\n        _TokenizingJSONObject((content, 1), True, fake_scan_once, {}, content)\n    assert excinfo.value.msg == \"Expecting ':' delimiter\"\n    assert excinfo.value.pos == 5\n\n\n@pytest.mark.parametrize(\"content,expected_pos\", [(\"{\\\"a\\\":\", 5), (\"{\\\"a\\\": \", 6)])\ndef test_truncated_after_colon(content, expected_pos):\n    with pytest.raises(JSONDecodeError) as excinfo:\n        _TokenizingJSONObject((content, 1), True, fake_scan_once, {}, content)\n    assert excinfo.value.msg == \"Expecting value\"\n    assert excinfo.value.pos == expected_pos\n\n\ndef test_multiple_whitespace_characters_after_colon():\n    content = '{\"a\":  1}'\n    result, end = _TokenizingJSONObject((content, 1), True, fake_scan_once, {}, content)\n    assert end == len(content) == 9\n    value = list(result.values())[0]\n    assert value.value == 1\n    assert (value.start, value.end) == (7, 7)\n\n\ndef test_missing_value_after_colon():\n    content = '{\"a\": }'\n    with pytest.raises(JSONDecodeError) as excinfo:\n        _TokenizingJSONObject((content, 1), True, fake_scan_once, {}, content)\n    assert excinfo.value.msg == \"Expecting value\"\n    assert excinfo.value.pos == 6\n\n\ndef test_missing_closing_brace():\n    content = '{\"a\":1'\n    with pytest.raises(JSONDecodeError) as excinfo:\n        _TokenizingJSONObject((content, 1), True, fake_scan_once, {}, content)\n    assert excinfo.value.msg == \"Expecting ',' delimiter\"\n    assert excinfo.value.pos == 6\n\n\ndef test_invalid_character_after_comma():\n    content = '{\"a\":1,}'\n    with pytest.raises(JSONDecodeError) as excinfo:\n        _TokenizingJSONObject((content, 1), True, fake_scan_once, {}, content)\n    assert excinfo.value.msg == \"Expecting property name enclosed in double quotes\"\n    assert excinfo.value.pos == 7\n\n\ndef test_comma_at_end_of_string():\n    content = '{\"a\":1,'\n    with pytest.raises(JSONDecodeError) as excinfo:\n        _TokenizingJSONObject((content, 1), True, fake_scan_once, {}, content)\n    assert excinfo.value.msg == \"Expecting property name enclosed in double quotes\"\n    assert excinfo.value.pos == 7\n```\n\nWait \u2014 for `test_comma_at_end_of_string`, pos: end-1 where end=8 \u2192 7. Yes.\n\nHmm, also for `{\"a\":1,}`: end after nextchar=',' is 7; line 88 end=_w(s,7).end()=7; nextchar=s[7:8]='}'; end=8; raise at end-1=7. Yes pos=7.\n\nNow, one thing to double check: in `test_missing_closing_brace` (`{\"a\":1`), after fake returns end=6, line 76: `nextchar = s[6]` \u2192 IndexError (len=6) \u2192 nextchar=''. end += 1 \u2192 7. Line 86: raise JSONDecodeError(\"Expecting ',' delimiter\", s, end - 1) \u2192 pos = 6. Yes.\n\nNow \u2014 the whitespace test: I should double check `scanstring` behavior with strict=True. `scanstring(s, end, strict)` \u2014 signature: `scanstring(string, end, strict=True)` \u2014 positional ok.\n\nAlso need to double-check: in `'{ \"a\" : 1 , \"b\" : 2 }'`, after first value at end=9, line 76: nextchar = s[9] = ' ' \u2192 in _ws \u2192 line 78: end = _w(s, 10).end() \u2192 WHITESPACE.match(s, 10) matches ' ' at 10 \u2192 end=11. nextchar = s[11] = ','. end += 1 \u2192 12. Line 86: nextchar == ',' \u2192 line 88: end = _w(s, 12).end() \u2192 matches ' ' at 12 \u2192 end=13. nextchar = s[13:14] = '\"'. end=14. Line 91: '\"' \u2192 continue. start = 13. scanstring(s, 14, True) \u2192 parses `\"b\"` \u2192 ('b', 16). \n\nWait, I made an indexing error earlier. Let me redo the indices for `{ \"a\" : 1 , \"b\" : 2 }`:\n\n```\n0: '{'\n1: ' '\n2: '\"'\n3: 'a'\n4: '\"'\n5: ' '\n6: ':'\n7: ' '\n8: '1'\n9: ' '\n10: ','\n11: ' '\n12: '\"'\n13: 'b'\n14: '\"'\n15: ' '\n16: ':'\n17: ' '\n18: '2'\n19: ' '\n20: '}'\n```\n\nlen = 21. OK so my earlier trace: after first key scanstring from end=3 \u2192 ('a', 5)? scanstring(s, 3, True): s[3] must be '\"'... wait no! scanstring expects s[end] == '\"'. At end=3, s[3]='a'. That's wrong!\n\nHold on. Let me re-trace. Call with (content, 1). end=1. nextchar = s[1] = ' ' \u2192 ws \u2192 end = _w(s,1).end() = 2. nextchar = s[2] = '\"'. Then `end += 1` \u2192 end = 3. Loop: start = end - 1 = 2. scanstring(s, 3, True) \u2014 s[3] = 'a'?? \n\nWait, scanstring(string, end) \u2014 the `end` parameter is the index of the opening quote? Let me check CPython's scanstring: `scanstring(s, end, strict=True)` \u2014 \"Scan the string s for a JSON string. End is the index of the character in s after the quote that started the string.\" So end points AFTER the opening quote. So scanstring(s, 3) with s[2]='\"' and s[3]='a' \u2192 parses 'a', closing quote at 4, returns ('a', 5). Yes! end param is after the opening quote. Good, my trace was right: scanstring(s, 3) \u2192 ('a', 5).\n\nSo key = ScalarToken('a', start=2, end=5-1=4). Good: (2,4).\n\nThen s[5:6] = ' ' != ':' \u2192 end = _w(s,5).end() = 6. s[6:7] = ':' \u2192 ok. end = 7. s[7] = ' ' in ws \u2192 end=8. s[8]='1' not ws. fake(s,8) \u2192 digit '1' \u2192 end=9 \u2192 (ScalarToken(1,8,8), 9). nextchar = s[9]=' ' in ws \u2192 end=_w(s,10).end()=11? \n\nWait: `_w(s, end + 1)` with end=9 \u2192 _w(s, 10). WHITESPACE.match(s, 10): s[10]=',' \u2014 WHITESPACE is `[ \\t\\n\\r]*` which matches empty at 10 (',' is not whitespace) \u2192 .end() = 10. So end = 10. nextchar = s[10] = ','. end += 1 \u2192 11.\n\nHmm wait, I need to be careful: line 78: `end = _w(s, end + 1).end()`. At that point end=9 (index of the space). end+1 = 10. _w(s,10) matches zero whitespace (s[10]=',') \u2192 end=10. nextchar = s[10] = ','. Then end += 1 \u2192 11. Line 86: nextchar==',' \u2192 line 88: end = _w(s, 11).end() \u2192 s[11]=' ' \u2192 matches ' ' \u2192 end=12. nextchar = s[12:13] = '\"'. end += 1 \u2192 13. Line 91: ok. Loop: start = 12. scanstring(s, 13, True) \u2192 s[12]='\"', s[13]='b' \u2192 ('b', 15). key = ScalarToken('b', 12, 14). \n\ns[15:16] = ' ' != ':' \u2192 end = _w(s,15).end() = 16. s[16]=':' \u2192 end=17. s[17]=' ' in ws \u2192 end=18. s[18]='2' not ws. fake(s,18) \u2192 (ScalarToken(2,18,18),19). nextchar=s[19]=' ' in ws \u2192 end=_w(s,20).end(): s[20]='}' \u2192 matches empty \u2192 end=20. nextchar=s[20]='}'. end += 1 \u2192 21. break. return (dict, 21). \n\nSo key positions: ('a': 2,4), ('b': 12,14). Values: 1 at (8,8), 2 at (18,18). end=21. My earlier assertions were right.\n\nNow the fast path test `{\"a\":1}`: indices 0='{',1='\"',2='a',3='\"',4=':',5='1',6='}'. Call (content,1): nextchar='\"' \u2192 end=2. start=1. scanstring(s,2) \u2192 ('a',4). key=ScalarToken('a',1,3). s[4:5]=':' \u2192 end=5. s[5]='1' not ws. fake(s,5) \u2192 (ScalarToken(1,5,5),6). nextchar=s[6]='}' \u2192 end=7 \u2192 break. return (dict,7). Good.\n\n`{\"a\":  1}`: indices 0='{',1='\"',2='a',3='\"',4=':',5=' ',6=' ',7='1',8='}'. end=5 after colon+1... trace: nextchar='\"' \u2192 end=2. scanstring(s,2)\u2192('a',4). s[4]=':' \u2192 end=5. s[5]=' ' ws \u2192 end=6. s[6]=' ' ws \u2192 line 66: end=_w(s,7).end() \u2192 s[7]='1' \u2192 empty match \u2192 end=7. fake(s,7) \u2192 (ScalarToken(1,7,7),8). nextchar=s[8]='}' \u2192 end=9. return end=9. Good.\n\nNow \u2014 potential issue: `s[idx].isdigit()` \u2014 for '1' fine. But also note `isdigit` on things like '\u00b2' (superscript two) returns True and int() would fail... not relevant for our test strings.\n\nNow, about StopIteration and `err.value`: `raise StopIteration(idx)` \u2192 caught as err \u2192 err.value == idx. Confirmed StopIteration stores first arg in `.value`. Actually let me double-check: \n\n```python\n>>> StopIteration(5).value\n5\n```\n\nYes \u2014 StopIteration.__new__/__init__ sets value from the first positional arg. This is documented behavior (PEP 380-ish, generator return values). Good.\n\n