w, the get_info calls. Let me also check `DictToken` in case I want an integration test. And check whether the module has `tokenize_json` and `_make_scanner`.\n\nActually, let me reconsider whether to include an integration test using the real scanner. Pros: validates that the function works with the real scan_once (which returns Tokens), covers lines via public API too. Cons: depends on module internals (`_make_scanner` signature). If get_info shows `_make_scanner(context)` exists, I can write:\n\n```python\ndef test_with_real_scanner():\n    from typesystem.tokenize.tokenize_json import _make_scanner\n\n    class Decoder:\n        strict = True\n        memo = {}\n\n    scan_once = _make_scanner(Decoder())\n    content = '{\"a\": 1, \"b\": [2, 3]}'\n    result, end = _TokenizingJSONObject((content, 1), True, scan_once, Decoder.memo, content)\n    ...\n```\n\nHmm, but the real scanner's JSONObject call passes context.memo... Actually in the real code, `_make_scanner`'s scanner calls `_TokenizingJSONObject((string, idx+1), context.strict, scan_once, context.memo, string)`. So context needs `.strict` and `.memo`. If I mimic that, fine.\n\nBut wait \u2014 there's a risk: if `_make_scanner` doesn't exist or has a different signature, the test breaks. get_info will tell me. Alternatively, use `json.scanner.py_make_scanner` with a context \u2014 that returns raw Python values (not Tokens), which still works for _TokenizingJSONObject (it just stores whatever scan_once returns). Actually that's a neat approach that doesn't depend on typesystem internals beyond the function under test:\n\n```python\nimport json\nfrom json.scanner import py_make_scanner\n\nclass Context:\n    strict = True\n\nscan_once = py_make_scanner(Context())\n```\n\npy_make_scanner(context) uses context.strict, context.parse_float, context.parse_int, context.parse_constant, context.object_hook, context.object_pairs_hook, memo... Let me recall py_make_scanner:\n\n```python\ndef py_make_scanner(context):