en`\n\nI'll do them in parallel.\n\nThen write the test file.\n\nLet me draft the test file:\n\n```python\nimport pytest\n\nfrom typesystem.tokenize.tokenize_json import _TokenizingJSONObject\nfrom typesystem.tokenize.tokens import ScalarToken\n\n\ndef fake_scan_once(s, idx):\n    \"\"\"Minimal stand-in for the real scanner: parses integer literals only.\"\"\"\n    if idx < len(s) and (s[idx].isdigit() or s[idx] == \"-\"):\n        ...\n```\n\nHmm, keep it simple: digits only.\n\n```python\ndef fake_scan_once(s, idx):\n    if idx >= len(s) or not s[idx].isdigit():\n        raise StopIteration(idx)\n    end = idx\n    while end < len(s) and s[end].isdigit():\n        end += 1\n    return ScalarToken(int(s[idx:end]), idx, end - 1, s), end\n```\n\nWait \u2014 but ScalarToken's constructor: `ScalarToken(value, start, end, content)`. Need to confirm. get_info will tell.\n\nNow the tests:\n\n```python\ndef test_object_fast_path():\n    content = '{\"a\":1}'\n    memo = {}\n    result, end = _TokenizingJSONObject((content, 1), True, fake_scan_once, memo, content)\n    assert end == len(content)\n    assert len(result) == 1\n    (key_token,) = result.keys()\n    assert key_token.value == \"a\"\n    assert (key_token.start, key_token.end) == (1, 3)\n    (value_token,) = result.values()\n    assert value_token.value == 1\n    assert (value_token.start, value_token.end) == (5, 5)\n```\n\nWait, key token: start=1 (the opening quote index), end = end-1 where end after scanstring = 4 \u2192 end-1 = 3 (closing quote index). So (1, 3). Value token from fake: start=5, end=5. Good.\n\nHmm, but does ScalarToken store start/end as given? Presumably `Token(value, start, end, content)`. Let me confirm via get_info.\n\n```python\ndef test_object_with_whitespace():\n    content = '{ \"a\" : 1 , \"b\" : 2 }'\n    result, end = _TokenizingJSONObject((content, 1), True, fake_scan_once, {}, content)\n    assert end == len(content)\n    keys = list(result.keys())\n    assert [k.value for k in keys] == [\"a\", \"b\"]\n    assert [result[k].value for k in keys] == [1, 2]\n    # key positions\n    assert (keys[0].start, keys[0].end) == (2, 4)\n    assert (keys[1].start, keys[1].end) == (12, 14)\n```\n\nLet me re-verify indices for `{ \"a\" : 1 , \"b\" : 2 }`:\n```\nindex: 0 '{'\n       1 ' '\n       2 '\"'\n       3 'a'\n       4 '\"'\n       5 ' '\n       6 ':'\n       7 ' '\n       8 '1'\n       9 ' '\n       10 ','\n       11 ' '\n       12 '\"'\n       13 'b'\n       14 '\"'\n       15 ' '\n       16 ':'\n       17 ' '\n       18 '2'\n       19 ' '\n       20 '}'\n```\nlen = 21.\n\nTrace with end=1:\n- nextchar = ' ' \u2192 ws \u2192 end = _w(s,1).end(). WHITESPACE = `[ \\t\\n\\r]*` matches ' ' at 1 \u2192 end=2. nextchar='\"'.\n- end=3, start=2. scanstring(s,3,True): parses `\"a\"` \u2192 returns ('a', 5). key=ScalarToken('a',2,4).\n- s[5:6]=' ' != ':' \u2192 end=_w(s,5).end()=6. s[6:7]=':' \u2192 ok. end=7.\n- s[7]=' ' in ws \u2192 end=8. s[8]='1' not ws.\n- fake_scan_once(s,8): '1' digit \u2192 parse '1' \u2192 end=9. returns (ScalarToken(1,8,8), 9).\n- nextchar = s[9]=' ' in ws \u2192 end=_w(s,10).end()=10. nextchar=s[10]=','. end=11.\n- ',' \u2192 line 88: end=_w(s,11).end()=12. nextchar=s[12]='\"'. end=13. ok.\n- start=12. scanstring(s,13,True) \u2192 ('b',15). key=ScalarToken('b',12,14).\n- s[15]=' ' != ':' \u2192 end=_w(s,15).end()=16. s[16]=':' \u2192 end=17.\n- s[17]=' ' in ws \u2192 end=18. s[18]='2' not ws.\n- fake(s,18) \u2192 (ScalarToken(2,18,18),19).\n- nextchar=s[19]=' ' in ws \u2192 end=_w(s,20).end()=20. nextchar=s[20]='}'. end=21.\n- break. return (dict, 21). \n\ndict keys: ScalarToken('a',2,4), ScalarToken('b',12,14). Values 1, 2.\n\nAssertions: end == 21, key values ['a','b'], value values [1,2], positions.\n\nThis covers: 37T, 38T, 39, 40, 48, 49-53, 56T, 57, 58F, 60, 63T, 64, 65F, 70-71, 74, 76, 77T, 78, 79, 84F (not '}'), 86F (','), 88, 89, 90, 91F, loop back.\n\n```python\ndef test_empty_object():\n    content = \"{}\"\n    resu